# {{ title }}

YAMD has built-in syntax for tracked changes, based on CriticMarkup. Every suggestion lives in the plain text of the note — reviewers can add them in any editor, and you can accept or reject them all in one command.

::toc{depth=2}

---

## Review markup syntax

| Markup | Meaning | Renders as |
| ------ | ------- | ---------- |
| `{++added text++}` | Insert | Green, underlined |
| `{--removed text--}` | Delete | Red, struck through |
| `{~~old~>new~~}` | Replace | Red old + green new |
| `{==highlighted==}{>>comment<<}` | Comment on text | Yellow highlight + margin note |
| `{>>standalone comment<<}` | Freestanding note | Margin note |

---

## A document under review

Here is a paragraph with all five kinds of markup applied:

The {++student++} {--user--} opens the {~~dashboard~>study dashboard~~} and sees
their {++upcoming++} deadlines.
{==The heatmap shows 26 weeks.==}{>>Is 26 the right default? — Reviewer<<}
Sessions are logged {++automatically++} to the daily note.
{>>Should we mention the `logToDailyNote` setting here? — Grace<<}

---

## Inline comments

Use `%% … %%` for hidden comments that are **never** shown in the output, not even in the HTML source. Good for TODO notes, personal reminders, or draft text you're not ready to delete.

```yamd
The mitochondrion %% add a citation here later %% is the powerhouse of the cell.

%%
TODO: expand this section before the final submission.
%%
```

---

## Accepting or rejecting all changes

Run these commands from the command palette while a `.yamd` file is open:

**YAMD: Accept All Review Changes**
- Applies every insertion, keeps the "new" side of replacements, removes
  deletion markup and all comments.
- The result is clean text with no markup.

**YAMD: Reject All Review Changes**
- Reverts to the original: removes insertions, restores deletions, keeps the
  "old" side of replacements, removes comments.
- The result is clean text with no markup.

Both commands make a single undoable edit (:kbd[Cmd+Z] to undo).

---

## Workflow for submitting work

A common workflow for a student submitting to a tutor or peer reviewer:

1. Write the draft in YAMD.
2. Share the `.yamd` file (Git, email, or a shared folder).
3. The reviewer adds `{++ ++}`, `{-- --}` and `{>> <<}` suggestions.
4. You open the file, read the comments in the preview, and use **Accept All** or **Reject All** — or accept selectively by editing the markup directly.

---

## Tips

- Review markup works anywhere in a note — paragraphs, headings, list items,
  table cells.
- Formatting works inside markup: `{++**bold** addition++}`.
- Comments are a great place for "sources:" notes while drafting, replaced with
  real citations before submission.
- The VS Code diff view (`git diff`) shows markup changes as source diffs;
  use `Accept All` to produce a clean version for the final commit.
